kosha functional-group

  -- the reactive site of an organic molecule. the part that determines chemistry.
  -- organic chemistry is fundamentally the study of functional groups:
  --   what they do, how they react, how they transform into each other.
  --
  -- a functional group is a specific arrangement of atoms that:
  --   1. is present across many different molecules
  --   2. reacts in a predictable way regardless of the rest of the molecule
  --   3. defines the class the molecule belongs to
  --
  -- the carbon skeleton (backbone) carries the functional groups.
  -- the backbone determines shape, size, and physical properties.
  -- the functional groups determine chemical reactivity.
  -- this separation — backbone + functional group — is the grammar of organic chemistry.
  --
  -- major functional groups (each has its own node):
  --   hydroxyl -OH     → alcohol. hydrophilic. hydrogen bonds. e.g. ethanol
  --   carboxyl -COOH   → carboxylic acid. donates H⁺. e.g. acetic acid, amino acids
  --   amino    -NH2    → amine. accepts H⁺ (basic). e.g. amino acids, amines
  --   carbonyl C=O     → aldehyde (terminal) or ketone (internal). reactive.
  --   ester    -COO-   → from acid + alcohol. fragrance, fats, plastics.
  --   ether    -O-     → between two carbons. low reactivity. solvent.
  --   thiol    -SH     → like hydroxyl but sulfur. disulfide bonds in proteins.
  --   phosphate -OPO3  → backbone of DNA/RNA, ATP energy currency.
  --   double bond C=C  → alkene. reactive. addition reactions. polymerisation.
  --   triple bond C≡C  → alkyne. very reactive. acetylene welding.

